    "agent_last_message": "I've built the word-count tool in `output/` (`CMakeLists.txt` and `main.cpp`). It compiles cleanly with `cmake -B build && cmake --build build` and uses only the C++ standard library.\n\n**What I tested:** a sample file with mixed case, punctuation, tabs, blank lines and no trailing newline gave the counts and word order I expected. An empty file, a missing input file and an unknown flag also behaved as intended (exit code 1 for the last two). I didn't test a file large enough to check speed.\n\n**How it follows the spec:**\n- Counts are by byte: every byte is one character, and a final line without a newline still counts as a line.\n- Words are split on the six whitespace characters the spec lists. Only A\u2013Z are lowercased, so accented letters keep their case. Punctuation stays part of the word.\n- `top_words` holds up to 10 words, sorted by count (highest first), with ties broken alphabetically.\n- Any special characters inside words are escaped so the JSON output is always valid.\n- Exit codes are 0 on success, 1 for invalid use or a bad input file, and 2 for internal errors such as running out of memory.\n\n**Choices you may want to change:**\n- **Invalid UTF-8 counts as \"malformed\".** The spec doesn't define a malformed file, so a file that isn't valid UTF-8 text is rejected with exit code 1. If you want it to accept any bytes instead, that's a one-line change.\n- **Repeating `--input` or `--output`, or giving it an empty value, also exits with 1.**\n- **The `--input=path` form is accepted** as well as `--input path`.\n- **If the output file can't be written, it exits with 1**, since the spec doesn't say which code to use there.",
